msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"A response surface design is an experimental framework used to optimize and "
"understand the relationships between several explanatory variables and one "
"or more response variables. By systematically varying the input variables, "
"researchers can explore the effects of these variables on the response, "
"identify optimal conditions, and understand the interactions between "
"variables. Response surface designs are particularly useful in situations "
"where the goal is to optimize a process or product, as they provide a "
"detailed map of the response landscape, enabling precise fine-tuning and "
"improvement."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"A response surface experiment extends the screening design to allow "
"estimation of quadratic effects. After resolving confounding, the screening "
"experiment allows coefficient estimates for main effects and interactions. "
"The response surface experiment expands this equation by adding quadratic or "
"even cubic terms. The form of the empirical model with quadratic terms is:"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"Estimating the coefficients of the model shown in the above equation "
"requires an experiment with at least three factor levels. A full factorial "
"experiment with three levels includes enough trials to estimate all "
"interactions, but quickly becomes large as the number of factors increases."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"Full factorial experiments include all interactions. The hierarchical "
"ordering principle and the effect heredity principle imply that higher order "
"interactions may be ignored, and that significant interactions usually occur "
"together with significant corresponding main effects. This allows accurate "
"predictions with 2nd or 3rd order polynomials which can be created with "
"significantly less trials than a full factorial experiment."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"*Central composite designs* (CCD) were first proposed by Box and Wilson for "
"building a second order (quadratic) model for the response variable without "
"needing to use a complete three-level factorial experiment. A central "
"composite design contains an embedded factorial (or fractional factorial of "
"resolution V) design with factorial points and centre points, augmented with "
"a group of star (axial) points that allows estimation of curvature. A "
"central composite design always contains twice as many star (or axial) "
"points (2k) as there are factors (k) in the design. These designs are "
"constructed from three main parts, each of which comprises a set of "
"experimental points:"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"The 2<sup>k</sup> vertices (±1, ±1, ..., ±1) of a *k*-dimensional \"cube\". "
"This part is simply a 2<sup>k</sup> factorial design or a fractional "
"factorial design. The variable coding is chosen so that the two levels (low "
"and high) are -1 or +1."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"The 2k vertices (±α, 0, ..., 0), (0, ±α, 0, ..., 0), ..., (0, ..., 0, ±α) of "
"a *k*-dimensional \"star\". These are pairs of experimental points on the "
"coordinate axes, all at a distance α from the origin. The quantity α is "
"often a value between 1.4 and 2, but depends on the number of variables and "
"the properties of the design under study. This part is often referred to as "
"\"star\" or \"axial\"."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "A number of centre points (0, 0, ..., 0)."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "The design shapes: cube, star, and cube plus star"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "There are three general types of central composite designs:"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "Central composite circumscribed (CCC)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"CCC designs are the original form of the central composite design. The star "
"(or axial) points are located on a circle encompassing the screening design. "
"These designs have circular, spherical, or hyper-spherical symmetry and "
"require five levels for each factor. It may not be possible to augment a "
"screening design, represented by the corners of the square, if the levels of "
"the screening design are absolute minimum and maximum, as the star (or "
"axial) points exceed these values."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "Face centred central composite (CCF)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"In the CCF design the star points are located at the centre of each face, "
"i.e. at the middle lines of the square (for two factor designs) or at the "
"centre of the square of the cube (for three factors) formed by the screening "
"design. This variety requires three levels of each factor. This design is "
"not rotatable, and has slightly less precision for coefficient estimation. "
"Its advantage is that star (or axial) points can be added to prior screening "
"designs."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "Central composite inscribed (CCI)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"For those situations in which the limits specified for factor settings are "
"truly limits, the CCI design uses star (or axial) points which are set at "
"the minimum and maximum levels. If augmenting a screening design, the levels "
"of the screening design will be within the absolute limits to allow the "
"expanded settings for the star (or axial) points. This design also requires "
"five levels of each factor."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"The types of central composite designs: circumscribed, face centred, and "
"inscribed"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"A *Box-Behnken design* (BBD) is an independent quadratic design in that it "
"does not contain an embedded factorial (or fractional factorial) design, "
"i.e. all factors are never set at their high levels simultaneously. In this "
"design the treatment combinations are at the midpoints of edges of the "
"process space and at the centre. These designs are rotatable (or near "
"rotatable) and require three levels of each factor. The designs have limited "
"capability for orthogonal blocking compared to the central composite designs."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid "A Box-Behnken design"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeResponseSurfaceMethodology|"
msgid ""
"The Box-Behnken set-up consists of twelve \"edge\" points (shown as red "
"dots) all lying on a single sphere about the centre of the experimental "
"region (shown as a blue dot), plus a few replicates of the centre point, "
"e.g., three (blue) centre points."
msgstr ""

